=== layout: one-panel

# *Multiplication Rules*

@body

- [*Specific Multiplication Rule*]{underline=true}, if two events, A and B, are [independent]{color=red}, then\:
  - [P(A and B) = P(A) &#215; P(B)]{color=red}
- [*General Multiplication Rule*]{underline=true}, if two events, A and B, are NOT independent then\:
  - [P(A and B) = P(A) &#215; P(B|A) = P(B) &#215; P(A|B)]{color=#00599D}

- [*When you hear &#8220;and&#8221; think MULTIPLICATION*]{color=green}

=== layout: one-panel

# *Addition Rules*

@body

- [*Specific Addition Rule\:*]{underline=true} if two events, A and B, are [mutually exclusive]{color=red}, then\:
  - [P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)]{color=red}
- [*General Addition Rule\:*]{underline=true} if two events, A and B, are NOT mutually exclusive, then\:
  - [P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) &#8211; P(A and B)]{color=#00599D}

- [*When you hear &#8220;or&#8221; think ADDITION*]{color=green}

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# Practice Question!

@body

- Imagine that we breed two dogs with the genotype&#160;_BbCc_, where dominant allele&#160;_B_&#160;specifies black coat color (versus&#160;_b_, yellow coat color) and dominant allele&#160;_C_&#160;specifies straight fur (versus&#160;_c_, curly fur). Assuming that the two genes assort independently and are not sex-linked, how can we predict the number of&#160;_BbCc_&#160;puppies among the offspring?
- What&#8217;s the probability of getting a&#160;_Bb_&#160;genotype?
- What&#8217;s the probability of getting an&#160;_Cc_&#160;genotype?

@notes

In order for a puppy to have a&#160;BbCc&#160;genotype, both of these events must take place: the puppy must receive&#160;Bb&#160;alleles,&#160;and&#160;it must receive&#160;Cc&#160;alleles. The two events are independent because the genes assort independently (don't affect one another's inheritance). So, once we calculate the probability of each genetic event, we can multiply these probabilities using the product rule to get the probability of the genotype of interest (BbCc).

